armed fighters in the democratic  republic of congo's north kibu province  take part in a ceremony  following their surrender to government  forces around 70 weapons mostly rifles  were also handed in  our work in the rebellion was to steal  and destroy the peace among the people  this is why we the leaders of the bush  troops decided to leave the bush  to bring our children out of the bush to  embrace a better life  the provinces of north kivu and itauri  in the east of drc were placed under  military rule last month  to stop escalating violence between  armed groups  president felix chisakedi put the army  and police in charge of administrative  court and policing affairs and two  generals were appointed as governors  replacing those who were elected  the cleanup operation is already  effective with several villages  recovered  we will continue to hunt down these  armed groups wherever they go  [Applause]  violence has plagued these eastern  provinces for years  with more than 100 armed groups  operating in the dense  forests and attacks on civilians has  surged recently  killing thousands of congolese and  displacing more than five million  so many support the president's actions  i feel very happy really happy to see  our cousins our brothers and uncles who  were once fighters  surrender since the military governor  has been in office  we see that the activism of the armed  groups is on the decline  now they are starting to surrender  without gunfire  president xi zakiri says his decision to  impose the so-called state of siege  was prompted by the suffering of those  living in north kivu and itauri  critics warned the security forces could  easily abuse their mandate  but supporters say military rule is  already making a difference  victoria gatenby al jazeera
 

